[[:db/retract 17592186113989 :answer-field/choices 17592186113990] [:db/retract 17592186113989 :answer-field/choices 17592186113991] [:db/retract 17592186113989 :answer-field/choices 17592186113992] [:db/retract 17592186113989 :answer-field/choices 17592186113993] [:db/retract 17592186113989 :answer-field/choices 17592186113994] [:db/retract 17592186113376 :answer-field/choices 17592186113377] [:db/retract 17592186113376 :answer-field/choices 17592186113378] [:db/retract 17592186113376 :answer-field/choices 17592186113379] [:db/retract 17592186113376 :answer-field/choices 17592186113380] [:db/retract 17592186113376 :answer-field/choices 17592186113381] {:db/id 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bb398bcb-fa77-5232-9d5f-318cb8cb3320" "question-73b54284-6d5f-5b76-a388-21fc0778bb8a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9e42534d-d33e-5203-bcb9-d3b1ae0266de" "question-4fc775b7-0196-5d35-b770-db9c71b04899" "question-682600e4-1f6c-58ba-ae45-caa8f795950e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115073 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/03/03d58fbe64af980e170a695b5f511df7e14cd74cc894e802ce5d781642d58b2b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/7b/7bb507644e3602983ed613dbe3b630fbd4fe72799c5412272a072a39c736cbc5.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cf425079-a50e-5390-afb0-f23f208d7e4f" "question-c2f3128b-e14c-5d17-b8ee-71ffa4c47e70"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115074 :question/problem "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](images/c2/c26c31fb0c03ed31508d71c394a0cac97706b8e98f495758ef1bc3cb7b9de37f.svg) and ![](images/27/2728c629af340140b991c75f58104833304f4dc82d6f2f04b29b6ddde363b349.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/11/11f12b427f63eb036d2f19210ab85c0a5b279bfda547031e8ae867d130aafc15.svg)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/4c/4c46704dbedb6bb10fd40107493f49481923b4f1e524dc8f3f84d98aa4f66256.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb398bcb-fa77-5232-9d5f-318cb8cb3320" :question/id #uuid "bb398bcb-fa77-5232-9d5f-318cb8cb3320" :question/math-academy-id "q-42704" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $M$, $N$ and $P$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-264fcad2-6268-5a65-8231-2261fb99ed94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-264fcad2-6268-5a65-8231-2261fb99ed94" :answer-field/id #uuid "264fcad2-6268-5a65-8231-2261fb99ed94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-081decf6-8b8b-5b4e-bc55-884af37deef0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac101c56-0025-5f6a-8d22-f432ce4b5ce6" "answer-7863b914-5ce1-519d-8bdf-4c54739802f9" "answer-58355426-69d0-55ca-a705-4a9d72d17bb3" "answer-609661d9-134c-5281-8a45-969f241f92a3" "answer-081decf6-8b8b-5b4e-bc55-884af37deef0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac101c56-0025-5f6a-8d22-f432ce4b5ce6" :answer/id #uuid "ac101c56-0025-5f6a-8d22-f432ce4b5ce6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$M$ and $N$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7863b914-5ce1-519d-8bdf-4c54739802f9" :answer/id #uuid "7863b914-5ce1-519d-8bdf-4c54739802f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$N$ and $P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58355426-69d0-55ca-a705-4a9d72d17bb3" :answer/id #uuid "58355426-69d0-55ca-a705-4a9d72d17bb3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-609661d9-134c-5281-8a45-969f241f92a3" :answer/id #uuid "609661d9-134c-5281-8a45-969f241f92a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-081decf6-8b8b-5b4e-bc55-884af37deef0" :answer/id #uuid "081decf6-8b8b-5b4e-bc55-884af37deef0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115839 :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $P$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrices $Q$ and $S$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ is an elementary matrix." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113971 :answer-field/correct 17592186113973 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-73b54284-6d5f-5b76-a388-21fc0778bb8a" :question/id #uuid "73b54284-6d5f-5b76-a388-21fc0778bb8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31889" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $D$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $F$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $E$ is an elementary matrix." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2a39083d-7da8-5ae0-912f-f708e52fb22e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a39083d-7da8-5ae0-912f-f708e52fb22e" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a39083d-7da8-5ae0-912f-f708e52fb22e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ca02a73-7d89-56f2-b95b-c2a98081117a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-781bf9fc-c8ed-589b-bc49-9fe5c4bb51ee" "answer-c42dc014-434e-5a33-be09-1ba19122dbc9" "answer-c82ee549-4efd-554c-bd79-6314d62f5a3e" "answer-5ca02a73-7d89-56f2-b95b-c2a98081117a" "answer-3156da43-688a-5c56-bb8a-3fd05e02f8ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-781bf9fc-c8ed-589b-bc49-9fe5c4bb51ee" :answer/id #uuid "781bf9fc-c8ed-589b-bc49-9fe5c4bb51ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D$ and $E$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c42dc014-434e-5a33-be09-1ba19122dbc9" :answer/id #uuid "c42dc014-434e-5a33-be09-1ba19122dbc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c82ee549-4efd-554c-bd79-6314d62f5a3e" :answer/id #uuid "c82ee549-4efd-554c-bd79-6314d62f5a3e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$E$ and $F$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ca02a73-7d89-56f2-b95b-c2a98081117a" :answer/id #uuid "5ca02a73-7d89-56f2-b95b-c2a98081117a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$E$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3156da43-688a-5c56-bb8a-3fd05e02f8ef" :answer/id #uuid "3156da43-688a-5c56-bb8a-3fd05e02f8ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e42534d-d33e-5203-bcb9-d3b1ae0266de" :question/id #uuid "9e42534d-d33e-5203-bcb9-d3b1ae0266de" :question/math-academy-id "q-73643" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c1b91bd-c02a-54c1-9144-7cdad3e92265"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c1b91bd-c02a-54c1-9144-7cdad3e92265" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c1b91bd-c02a-54c1-9144-7cdad3e92265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ff5fc1a-d5a0-51b3-8cf3-52c45c688cc0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b9d9eea-15de-5c1d-ae98-366b6c4fc7bc" "answer-0d928832-0507-5fdf-88b2-89972940e5e3" "answer-1255b6e9-ff95-561b-b9f3-8ded22368554" "answer-702fbf57-e626-5e53-9e98-7e555e45d6ed" "answer-9ff5fc1a-d5a0-51b3-8cf3-52c45c688cc0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b9d9eea-15de-5c1d-ae98-366b6c4fc7bc" :answer/id #uuid "8b9d9eea-15de-5c1d-ae98-366b6c4fc7bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d928832-0507-5fdf-88b2-89972940e5e3" :answer/id #uuid "0d928832-0507-5fdf-88b2-89972940e5e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1255b6e9-ff95-561b-b9f3-8ded22368554" :answer/id #uuid "1255b6e9-ff95-56